  Adding Poisson Disc sampling to QMC
Hi @tupui,

I have looked at the class definition for QMC engines, and I'm having some trouble adapting Bridson's algorithm for PD to these definitions.

As far as I see, I should be able to produce an arbitrary number of samples when `random (n)` is called. There are two issues there due to the nature of the distribution:

1. Since every new sample excludes a sphere around itself from any further sampling, there is a finite number of samples that can be drawn from a region of space for a given minimum distance between points _r_. What should the function do if it can produce no more samples?
2. Bridson's algorithm exploits the properties of a maximum PD distribution to produce new points efficiently. Specifically, it draws new points only in an annulus _[r, 2r]_ away from an existing point. This means, that if we draw PD points sequentially, we will densely fill one region of space first, instead of drawing uniformly in the [0; 1) interval. One way that I see to circumvent this is to generate the entire distribution on `__init__`, and then to return random samples from this distribution each time `random(n)` is called. Is this approach acceptable for the library?
  </issue>
  
